.TH DEEPMON-KEY 1 "2026-11-01" "deepmon 1.0.10" "User commands"
.SH NAME
deepmon-key \- manage the DeepSeek API key and the platform session token
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B deepmon-key
.I command
.RB [ \-\-platform ]
.RB [ \-\-stdin ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Secrets are stored in the GNOME keyring. The API key is kept with the attributes
application=deepmon, account=deepseek-api; the platform web session token with
account=deepseek-platform. The extension reads them from there. The fallback for
the API key is the file ~/.config/deepmon/key with mode 0600.
.PP
A secret is read from standard input without echo and never passed as a command
line argument, so it does not appear in shell history or in the process list.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B set [--platform] [--stdin]
Store the secret in the keyring. In a terminal the input is hidden; when input is
redirected, the \fB--stdin\fR flag is required.
.TP
.B status
Show the source (keyring or file) and the sha256 fingerprint of both secrets.
.TP
.B fingerprint [--platform]
Print the fingerprint only.
.TP
.B delete [--platform]
Remove the secret from the keyring. Files under ~/.config/deepmon are not touched.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --platform
Operate on the platform web session token instead of the API key. The token
allows importing daily costs from platform.deepseek.com through a private
endpoint; it grants full access to the account.
.TP
.B --stdin
Read the secret from standard input instead of prompting.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments, invalid key or token format.
.TP
.B 3
Keyring error, or the terminal echo could not be disabled (use \fB--stdin\fR).
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
  deepmon-key set
  deepmon-key set --platform
  deepmon-key status
  pass show deepseek | deepmon-key set --stdin
.fi
